course: religion-ethics
lesson: 41
title: Dilemmas about truth and friendship
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A dilemma is a choice where two good things pull in different directions, such as being honest and being loyal to a friend.
explain: Imagine your friend knocks a vase off the teacher's desk, and the teacher asks the class who did it. You want to tell the truth, and you also want to stand by your friend.
explain: A good answer to a dilemma does not just say "because"; it gives reasons, looks at who could be hurt, weighs the other side and treats every person with respect.
explain: Christians believe truth and love belong together: the Bible speaks of "speaking the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15), so honesty should be gentle and kindness should be honest.
explain: For a child today this means: loyalty is good, but not loyalty to wrongdoing; secrets that put someone in danger must be told to a trusted adult; and a real friend never asks you to lie for them.
fact: A dilemma is a hard choice between two things that both seem right, or two things that both seem wrong.
fact: Loyalty means standing by your friends, family and groups, but loyalty to wrongdoing is not a virtue.
fact: Lying to protect a friend who did wrong usually lets someone else take the blame and stops the friend from putting things right.
fact: A good way through is often to encourage the friend to own up first, and to give them a chance before you tell.
fact: The "murderer at the door" is a famous puzzle: should you lie to someone who wants to harm your friend hiding inside?
fact: Most people think lying to the murderer is right because saving a life matters more; the philosopher Immanuel Kant argued that even this lie is wrong (disputed).
fact: A surprise is a happy secret that will soon be told; a dangerous secret hides harm and should be told to a trusted adult.
fact: Telling tales is telling just to get someone into trouble; reporting harm is telling to protect someone.
fact: A useful question is: am I telling to help someone or to hurt someone?
fact: Christians believe God is truthful and wants people to be honest too; one of the Ten Commandments forbids lying about others.
fact: Christians believe in "speaking the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15): honest words said gently and with care.
fact: Peer pressure is feeling pushed by friends to do something you think is wrong; real friends respect your choices.
fact: A promise creates a duty; breaking it just because something more fun came along lets the other person down.
fact: Some promises can be outweighed by a more serious duty, such as helping someone who is hurt, and then you should explain and apologise.
fact: Covering for a friend means lying that they were with you; it can hurt the people who are fooled, the friend, and you.
exercise: What is a dilemma? | A hard choice between two good things | A puzzle that always has one easy answer | A rule that everybody has to obey at school | A story that has no lesson in it at all
exercise: Your friend broke a vase and the teacher asks if you know who did it. What is the best-reasoned first step? | Urge your friend to own up, because someone else may be blamed | Say nothing, because friends always come first no matter what | Tell the teacher at once and shout it so everyone knows the friend is clumsy | Blame a child you dislike, because then nobody you like gets into trouble
exercise: Why can lying to protect a friend who did wrong be unfair? | Someone innocent may be blamed, and the friend never puts it right | It is not unfair, since the vase was only a small thing | It is unfair only if the teacher finds out about the lie | It is unfair because teachers are always right about everything that happens
exercise: Which kind of loyalty is a good thing? | Standing by a friend while still refusing to help them do wrong | Doing anything your friend says because they are your friend | Agreeing with your group even when they are cruel to others | Keeping every secret, even one that puts someone in danger
exercise: Can loyalty ever be a bad thing? | Yes, when it means helping someone hide or keep doing wrong | No, loyalty is always good whatever the friend has done | Yes, loyalty is always bad because friends cannot be trusted | No, because loyal people never have to make hard choices
exercise: In the "murderer at the door" puzzle, why do most people think the lie is allowed? | Because saving a friend's life matters more than the duty not to lie | Because lying is never wrong when you are frightened of someone | Because the murderer is a bad person, so his feelings and wishes do not count | Because nobody would ever find out that you had told a lie
exercise: Which philosopher argued that even lying to the murderer at the door is wrong? | Immanuel Kant | Isaac Newton | Charles Dickens | William Shakespeare
exercise: What is the difference between a surprise and a dangerous secret? | A surprise is soon told; a dangerous secret hides harm | A surprise is always bigger than any secret could ever be | A dangerous secret is just a surprise that lasts a long time | There is no difference, so both must always be kept forever
exercise: A friend says, "Promise you won't tell, my uncle hits me." What is the best-reasoned answer? | Tell a trusted adult, because a secret that hides harm keeps the friend in danger | Keep the promise, because a promise is a promise and that is the end of it | Tell the whole class, because more people knowing will scare the uncle | Do nothing, because what happens in another family is never your business
exercise: What is the difference between telling tales and reporting harm? | Telling tales aims to get someone in trouble; reporting aims to protect | Telling tales is about big things and reporting is about small things | Reporting harm is always done in secret and telling tales is always done out loud | There is no difference, since both mean telling a teacher something
exercise: What question helps you decide whether to tell a teacher? | Am I telling to help someone or to hurt someone? | Will telling make me the teacher's favourite? | Is the person I am telling about my friend? | Will anybody find out that it was me who told?
exercise: Your friend asks what you think of their story, and it needs a lot of work. What is the best answer? | Praise what is good, then gently suggest one or two things to improve | Say it is perfect, because the truth might upset them a little | Say it is rubbish, because honesty means saying exactly what you feel | Refuse to read it, because then you never have to say anything
exercise: What does "speaking the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15) mean? | Being honest in a gentle, caring way | Only telling the truth to the people you love the most | Saying loving things even when they are not really true | Telling the truth loudly so that everybody can hear it
exercise: Friends say, "Come on, everyone's taking sweets from the shop, don't be a baby." What is the best-reasoned reply? | Say no calmly, because stealing harms the shop | Go along with it, because being left out would be worse than the stealing | Call them losers, because people who steal deserve to be insulted | Take some, because it only counts as wrong if you are the only one
exercise: You promised to play with Sam on Saturday, then Leo invites you to a great party. What is the best-reasoned choice? | Keep the promise to Sam, or ask Sam honestly, because he is counting on you | Go to the party and say nothing, because a party is more fun than playing with Sam | Go to the party because Sam is a boring person anyway | Do whatever, because promises between children do not really matter
exercise: When might a promise rightly be outweighed? | When a more serious duty comes up, like helping someone who is hurt | Whenever a better offer comes along that you would enjoy more | Whenever you are a bit tired and do not feel like doing it | Never, a promise must be kept even if someone gets badly hurt
exercise: Your friend asks you to say he was at your house when he was not. Who could be hurt? | His parents who are fooled, your friend, and you who lied | Nobody, because a small lie for a friend cannot hurt anyone | Only you, because you are the one who has to say the words | Only the friend's parents, and they would never find out
test: Your little brother says, "Lying is always wrong, even to a burglar asking where Mum keeps her purse." How do you best answer? | Most people think lying to stop harm or robbery is allowed | He is right, and you must tell the burglar exactly where the purse is | Lying is fine whenever you feel like it, so the question is silly | You should ignore him, because little brothers never have good ideas
test: Your mate texts: "ur not gonna grass me up about the vase r u?? we're mates." What is the best reasoned reply? | Mates, yes, but someone else might get blamed, so own up | Course not, mates never tell on mates whatever happens | Shut up, you're an idiot and I'm telling everyone right now | Only if you give me your new game, then I'll keep quiet
test: A classmate who is not religious asks, "Why do Christians care so much about not lying?" What is the most respectful, accurate answer? | Christians believe God is truthful and wants people to be honest too | Because Christians think people who are not religious always lie | Because Christians are never allowed to change their mind about anything | Christians do not care about lying, it is just a rule for church
test: Your sister says, "Ella told me a secret but it's a surprise party for Mum, so I should tell Mum to be honest." What do you say? | A happy surprise soon told is fine to keep | She must tell Mum, because every secret is a kind of lie | She should tell the whole family, since surprises are dangerous | She should tell Ella's teacher, because secrets must be reported
test: Jack moans, "Freya told Miss that Ollie was pushing the little kids. Total snitch." Is Jack right? | No, Freya reported harm to protect the little ones | Yes, telling a teacher about anything always makes you a snitch | Yes, because Freya only did it to get Ollie into trouble for fun | No, but only because Freya is the teacher's favourite anyway
test: Your friend reads you her poem and asks, "Be honest, is it good?" It rhymes badly. Which reply best mixes honesty and kindness? | I love the idea; some rhymes feel forced, want to fix them together? | It's the best poem ever written, don't change a single word | It's terrible, honestly, you should give up writing poems | Whatever, I don't really care about poems, ask someone else
test: Kids at the bus stop: "Dare ya to throw a stone at that car. Go on, chicken." What is the best-reasoned response? | Say no, because it could hurt someone, and dares don't make it OK | Do it, because being called chicken is worse than any damage | Throw it but miss on purpose, so you look brave and nobody is hurt | Push the kid who dared you, because he deserves it for being so annoying
test: Dad asks, "Were you with Tom all afternoon?" Tom begged you to say yes, but he was at the river alone. What is the best choice? | Tell the truth, because his parents need to know he may be in danger | Say yes, because Tom is your best friend and he asked you really nicely | Say you can't remember, so you are not really lying | Say yes, and then tell Tom he owes you a big favour
test: A judge at the olympiad asks: "Isn't loyalty always good?" What is the best-reasoned answer? | Loyalty is good, but loyalty to wrongdoing hurts people, so it has limits | Yes, loyalty is always good, because the Bible says so and that ends the matter | No, loyalty is silly, because you can never trust any friend | Yes, because if you are not loyal people will call you names
test: A Muslim classmate says, "In my family we also keep promises very seriously." What is the best response? | That's true for us too; many faiths teach that promises create real duties | That's impossible, only Christians care about keeping promises they have made | That's nice but your promises do not count as real promises | Who cares, promises are only important at church
test: You promised to help Gran on Saturday, but your football team gets a surprise match. What is the best-reasoned approach? | Talk to Gran honestly; keep the promise unless she truly agrees to change the day | Go to the match and tell Gran you were ill, so she is not upset | Skip both, because if you cannot do both you should do neither | Go to the match because Gran is old and will soon forget
test: Your cousin says, "Kant said lying is always wrong, so everyone agrees on the murderer at the door." What is wrong with this? | Kant held that, but most people think the lie is allowed | Kant never wrote about lying, so the cousin made it up | Kant said lying is always right, which is the opposite | Nothing is wrong, everyone agrees with Kant about this puzzle
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a moral dilemma? | A situation wherein two obligations, each apparently binding, cannot both be fulfilled | A situation wherein a single rule is obeyed without any difficulty whatsoever | A situation wherein the agent possesses no obligations of any kind | A situation wherein the correct course of action is established by a majority vote
exam: Identify the principal distinction between the reporting of harm and the telling of tales. | The former is undertaken to protect a person; the latter to procure another's punishment | The former concerns trivial and petty matters; the latter concerns grave and serious matters | The former is invariably anonymous; the latter is invariably public | The former is permitted only to adults; the latter only to minors
exam: Given that a friend has confided a secret which conceals a danger to that friend, select the response most consistent with sound ethical reasoning. | Disclosure to a trusted adult, the friend's safety outweighing the confidence | Preservation of the confidence in all circumstances, a promise being absolute | Publication of the matter to the entire class without delay | Abstention from any action, the matter lying beyond the agent's concern
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the passage "speaking the truth in love" (Ephesians 4:15) is consistent with Christian teaching. | Honesty ought to be exercised with gentleness and regard for the welfare of the hearer | Honesty is required solely in respect of persons whom the speaker loves | Falsehood is permissible wheresoever it is expressed affectionately | Truth ought to be declared loudly and in public irrespective of its effect upon others
